  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Friday)\nIssued at 1118 AM CST SAT NOV 21 2026\n\nShowers will remain in the forecast today as the disturbance to\nour east keeps moving to the NW. The pressure gradient around\nthis disturbance remains relatively tight, meaning gusty winds\nwill remain in the forecast through today as well. Both of these\nactivities will begin to decrease overnight, with a weak ridge\nbuilding in overhead for Sunday. However, to our west, a trough\nand cold front will be working through the Plains, eventually\nworking into the Midwest by late Monday evening. We're currently\nexpected to be on the southern end of this trough, with the cold\nfront pushing through during the day on Monday. This cold front\nwill allow for additional rain chances in our NW zones Monday\nmorning, but rainfall amounts are expected to be low.\n\nThe next system in the forecast period will begin to take shape\non Tuesday, as a deep trough becomes cut off somewhere over\ncentral Texas. This closed low will continue to move east, before\ngetting swept back up into the main flow aloft sometime on\nWednesday. This closed low will quickly turn flow back to the\nsouth, allowing for a brief stint of moisture return ahead of it.\nGiven the ample forcing, rain will return to the forecast for\nmost of the day on Wednesday, before this low works out of the\nregion. Cold air will quickly get pulled down behind this low as\nwell, with highs on Thanksgiving proper expected to be in the mid-\n50s to low-60s. Thankfully, confidence is medium that this system\nwill completely clear the region by Thanksgiving, with no\nrainfall currently in the forecast for Thursday or Friday.\n\n/44/",
